# Commit messages

Lead with a **bracketed area tag** so `git log --oneline` readers know the domain immediately, then a concise what/why.

## Format

```
[area] Short summary of what changed and why.

Optional body for context. Link the issue with `Refs #N`. The project board closes it when the work merges to `stage`.
```

## Area tags (pick the best fit)

| Tag | Use for |
|-----|---------|
| `[DX]` | Tooling, configs, Cursor rules, lint/format, hooks |
| `[DX:web]` | DX change scoped to the web app |
| `[web:marketing]` | Marketing UI / landing routes |
| `[web:auth]` | Auth/signin surfaces |
| `[web]` | Other web app work |
| `[ui]` | Shared `packages/ui` components |
| `[DEPLOY]` / `[STAGING]` | Deploy pipeline or staging-only infra |
| `[BRANDING]` | Product naming / brand assets |

Use `[area:subarea]` when it helps (e.g. `[DX:web]`, `[web:marketing]`).

## Examples (from this repo)

- `[DX] Document GitHub auth caveat for hereisphil vs phil-hgraph token.`
- `[web:marketing] WhyMnemonics.tsx - put h1/p atop of flashcarddemo, better ui/ux, especially for mobile.`
- `[DX] Created .cursor/rules and added basic .mdc files to assist the Cursor IDE's AI agents.`

## Do

- Prefer present/past tense consistency with nearby commits; keep the subject readable without opening the diff
- Mention the *reason* when the diff is large for a mechanical reason (e.g. ESLint return-type annotations)
- Add `Refs #N` in the commit or pull request body. Issues stay open through Review and close when they merge to `stage`
- Keep `Closes #N` off pull requests into `dev`. GitHub only auto-closes on the default branch, `main`, which would skip the staging review
- When a commit completes one or more tasks on a linked issue, check those boxes in the issue body in the same turn (`- [ ]` → `- [x]`). Check only the tasks that commit finishes. Leave the issue open and leave every other task unchecked
- Apply issue, milestone, label, and project edits with the `gh` CLI in the same turn. Do not ask for review or approval first, and do not use a GitHub tool that waits on an approval card. These edits are reversible

## GitHub edits

When the task needs `gh` to change an issue, milestone, or similar GitHub metadata, run the edit directly. The user does not review those changes before they land.

This private repo is `hereisphil/2606-WDV349-CantuPhillip`. A `GITHUB_TOKEN` or `GH_TOKEN` on this machine can force the `phil-hgraph` login, which cannot see the repo. Unset both, and use the `hereisphil` account:

```bash
env -u GITHUB_TOKEN -u GH_TOKEN gh issue view 46 --repo hereisphil/2606-WDV349-CantuPhillip
```

## Do not

- Vague subjects like "updates" or "fix stuff"
- Skip the `[area]` prefix on feature/fix commits
